.TH AI-REVIVE 1 "September 2026" "ai-revive 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ai-revive \- save and restore a tmux/byobu workspace across reboots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ai-revive
.RI [ \-q ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B byobu-ai-revive
.RI [ \-q ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ai-revive
snapshots every tmux session, window, pane, working directory and layout of the running
server and rebuilds them on a fresh server, typically the one byobu starts after a reboot.
For each pane it asks a chain of
.I resumers
(see
.BR ai-revive-resumers (7))
whether the program running there can be relaunched with its state: Claude Code conversations
come back with
.B claude --resume
.IR session-id ,
ssh connections with their original command line, and any program listed in
.B RESUME_COMMANDS
with its arguments.
.PP
Snapshots are written to
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/ai-revive/snapshots/ ,
the newest one being
.IR last.json .
Saving happens on tmux events (new window, split, rename, layout change, ...) through hooks,
periodically through a systemd user timer, on
.B prefix + C-s
and, most reliably, through
.B ai-revive reboot
and
.BR "ai-revive shutdown" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR save " [" \-\-debounce "] [" \-\-force ]
Write a snapshot of the running server.
.B \-\-debounce
waits
.B SAVE_DEBOUNCE_S
seconds and coalesces a burst of events (what the hooks use).
Without
.BR \-\-force ,
a snapshot of a freshly started server (one window, one pane, nothing running) never replaces
a richer previous snapshot, and no snapshot is written while a restore is in progress.
.TP
.BR restore " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \-\-layout\-only "] [" \-\-auto "] [" \fIsnapshot\fR ]
Rebuild the sessions of
.I snapshot
(default:
.IR last )
on the running server. Windows that already exist are skipped, so restoring twice is harmless.
The placeholder session tmux or byobu created on a fresh start is reused and renamed.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints the plan and touches nothing;
.B \-\-layout\-only
rebuilds windows and panes without relaunching programs;
.B \-\-auto
is what the plugin runs at server start: it obeys
.BR AUTO_RESTORE ,
runs at most once per server and stays silent.
.TP
.B list
Snapshots on disk, newest first, with their boot id and a summary.
.TP
.B status
Where ai-revive is enabled, whether the hooks and the timer are active, the newest snapshot.
.TP
.BR enable " [" \-\-byobu | \-\-tmux ]
Wire the plugin into the user's byobu configuration
.RI ( $BYOBU_CONFIG_DIR/.tmux.conf ,
sourced last by byobu) or into
.IR ~/.tmux.conf ,
install the hooks on a running server and enable the systemd user timer. The default flavour
is byobu when byobu is installed or the command was invoked as
.BR byobu-ai-revive .
The configuration change is a marker-delimited block that
.B disable
removes again.
.TP
.B disable
Undo
.BR enable .
.TP
.BR resumers " [" list "|" probe " \fIpane-id\fR]"
List the effective resumers, or show which one claims a live pane and what it would run.
.TP
.BR shutdown ", " reboot
Save with
.B \-\-force
and then run
.B systemctl poweroff
or
.BR "systemctl reboot" .
This is the reliable way to power off: the shutdown hook installed by
.B enable
runs when the user session ends, but systemd does not guarantee that the tmux server is still
alive at that moment.
.TP
.BR version ", " help
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing on stderr; the log in
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ai-revive/ai-revive.log
is always written.
.SH HOW A REBOOT WORKS
.IP 1. 4
.B ai-revive enable
once. From then on every layout change and every 5 minutes produce a snapshot.
.IP 2. 4
.BR "ai-revive reboot" ,
or any other reboot: the newest snapshot is at most a few minutes old.
.IP 3. 4
Log in and start
.BR byobu .
Its configuration runs
.IR ai-revive.tmux ,
which installs the hooks and starts
.B ai-revive restore \-\-auto
in the background. The snapshot is from a previous boot, so the windows, names, layouts and
directories come back within a second, and each pane's program is typed into its shell once the
prompt is up: the Claude panes resume their exact conversations, ssh panes reconnect.
.IP 4. 4
A second
.B byobu
in another terminal attaches through byobu's usual grouped session; nothing is restored twice.
.SH TMUX WITHOUT BYOBU
Add
.B set -g @plugin 'bumml/ai-revive'
to
.I ~/.tmux.conf
and install it with TPM, or run
.BR "ai-revive enable \-\-tmux" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ai-revive/config
Settings, see
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai-revive/examples/config .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ai-revive/resumers/
User resumers; a file with the same name as a shipped one replaces it.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ai-revive/snapshots/
Snapshots and
.IR last.json .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ai-revive/
Per-boot markers and locks.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Every configuration key can be set as
.BI AI_REVIVE_ KEY
and takes precedence over the file.
.B AI_REVIVE_NO_RESTORE=1
disables the automatic restore for one server start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai-revive-resumers (7),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR byobu (1)
